[{:db/id "q-124142/selection/f47bf9ed14382ee552e9cbecde11c3fc18a20d53784e9ae06dd7847aa92ee544" :answer/id #uuid "39ca8b96-393e-51a0-8a43-c128630fd085" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(k+2)\\cdot k!" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186177574 :answer-field/choices ["q-124142/selection/f47bf9ed14382ee552e9cbecde11c3fc18a20d53784e9ae06dd7847aa92ee544"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-124142"] :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of factorial, we have \n\n$$\n(k+2)!=(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+2)! & =(k+2)\\cdot \\overset{(k+1)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)!\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-19874"] :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n(n+6)!=(n+6)(n+5)(n+4)(n+3)!\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $(n+5)$ and $(n+3)!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n+6)!}{(n+5)\\text{ }(n+3)!} & =\\frac{(n+6)\\cdot (n+5)\\cdot (n+4)\\cdot (n+3)!}{(n+5)\\cdot (n+3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+6)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+5)}\\cdot (n+4)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+3)!}}{\\cancel{(n+5)}\\cdot \\cancel{(n+3)!}} \\\\  & =(n+6)\\cdot (n+4) \\\\  & ={n}^{2}+10n+24\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
